[Proposition prop:degree-three-passage of arXiv:2608.23545, 'Eulerian walkers on $\Z^2$ have range exponent $2/3$'] Let $G$ be a doubly periodic graph in $\mathbb R^2$ of maximum degree three, equipped with an
		arbitrary rotor mechanism and independent uniform initial rotors.
		There are constants $c,C>0$ such that, for every directed edge $u\to v$ and
		every integer $R\geq1$,
		\begin{equation}
			\mathbb P\left\{
			\begin{array}{c}
				\text{a live path starts with $u\to v$ and contains a vertex}\\
				\text{at graph distance $R$ from $u$}
			\end{array}
			\right\}
			\leq Ce^{-cR}\, .
		\end{equation}

