[Theorem thm:lin-SDE of arXiv:2608.19198, 'Probabilistic Representation and Convergence of Gromov-Wasserstein Gradient Flows'] Let $(A_t)_{t \in [0,\infty)}$ be the solution of the matrix ODE (equation eq:IVP of the paper). The IGW gradient flow $(\rho_t)_{t \in [0,\infty)}$ agrees with the unique time-marginal flow of the linear SDE
\begin{equation}
    dX_t = -\frac14\big(A_t^{-1}+A_t^{-2}\big)X_t\,dt + A_t^{-1/2}\,dW_t, \quad
    X_0\sim \rho_0,
\end{equation}
where $(W_t)_{t \in [0,\infty)}$ is a standard Brownian motion in $\mathbb{R}^d$ independent of $X_0$. That is, $\rho_t=\mathrm{Law}(X_t)$ for all $t \in [0,\infty)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary lem:5.2 of arXiv:2412.01489, 'One- and two-particle spectral gap identities for the symmetric inclusion process and rela'] For all integers $k\ge 2$, we have
\begin{equation}
\lim_{\varepsilon \to 0} \frac{{\rm gap}_k( \varepsilon \hat\alpha)}{\varepsilon}= w_k(\hat\alpha)\; \text{.}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Cons 7 of arXiv:2606.08115, '2606.08115'] Suppose for two vectors $\ov{x}, \ov{y} \in K$ that $\ov{x} \le \ov{y}$. Then we have
\begin{equation}

V\left(M\ov{x}\right) \le V\left(M\ov{y}\right).
\end{equation}
\begin{proof}
Note that the inequality $\ov{x} \le \ov{y}$ entails $m_n\left(\ov{x}\right) \le m_n\left(\ov{y}\right)$ for every natural number $n$, where the function $m_n$ is defined by (\ref{mn_def}). From this inequality and relation (\ref{e}), we get the desired inequality.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:truncated_expectation_diff_ineq_at_doubling_scales of arXiv:2508.18809, '{\bf Critical long-range percolation III: The upper critical dimension}'] If $\alpha<d$ then there exists a positive constant $C$ such that
\begin{multline*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial r} \mathbb E_{\beta,r} |K| 
\geq \beta |J'(r)|  \mathbb E_{\beta,r}\left[ \sum_{y\in B_r} |K| \min\{|K_y|,m\} \mathbbm{1}(0 \nleftrightarrow y) \right]\\
\geq \beta |B_r||J'(r)| \mathbb E_{\beta,r} |K| \left(\mathbb E \min\{|K|,m\} - 
C r^{-(d-\alpha)/2}m \right)
\end{multline*}
for every $0<\beta\leq \beta_c$ and $r,m\geq 1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem: Minimizer of upper bounded expectation of arXiv:2411.10271, '$A$-localized states for clock models on trees and their extremal decomposition into glass'] Let $h: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ be a function defined as
\begin{equation*}
    h(t):=e^{-t\delta_0|\gamma|}(f(\beta)e^{t}+1)^{1+d|\gamma|}
\end{equation*}
for all $t\in \mathbb{R}$. If $\beta$ is small enough, $h$ possesses a unique minimizer 
    \begin{equation}
        t^*=\log\left(\frac{\delta_0 |\gamma|}{(1+d|\gamma|)f(\beta)-\delta_0|\gamma|f(\beta)}\right)
    \end{equation}
    where the quantity $\delta_0$ is introduced in (equation eq: Definition delta_0 of the paper) and the function $f$ in Lemma (lem: Propagation lemma of the paper).

