[Thm Theorem:DualOperator-L* of arXiv:2411.13765, 'Schr\"odinger Bridge Problem for Jump Diffusions'] Assume $L$ is a jump-diffusion operator given by (equation ReferenceGenerator of the paper) with coefficients satisfying the Assumptions \textup{(Assumptions-(C) of the paper)}. Then, the $L^2([0,T]\times\mathbb{R}^n)$-adjoint operator $L^*: C^{\infty}_0 \to C^{\infty}_b$ is well defined, and for every $f\in C_c^\infty([0,T]\times\mathbb{R}^n)$ and $(t,x)\in[0,T]\times\mathbb{R}^n$,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
     L^*f(t,x)&:= -\nabla \cdot (bf) (t,x)+\frac{1}{2} \sum_{i,j}\frac{\partial^2[(\sigma\sigma^T)_{ij}f]}{\partial x_i \partial x_j}(t,x) \\
    &\;+\int_{\mathbb{R}^\ell} \left[\det \nabla\phi_{t,z}^{-1}(x)f(t,\phi_{t,z}^{-1}(x))-f(t,x) +\mathbbm{1}_{|z|\leq 1}\nabla\cdot (\gamma f)(t,x,z) \right]\nu(dz),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\det \nabla\phi_{t,z}^{-1}$ is the Jacobian determinant of $\phi_{t,z}^{-1}$.

[Theorem 7 of arXiv:2512.02855, 'Loewner--Kufarev entropy and large deviations of the Hastings--Levitov model'] Let $\rho \in \mathcal{N}$. Then
    \begin{align}
    \cH(\rho) \le 2 S(\rho).
\end{align}
In particular, if $K$ is any compact hull bounded by a Weil--Petersson quasicircle, then $K$ can be generated by a measure with finite Loewner--Kufarev entropy. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition G_causal_definition_2 of arXiv:2608.13716, 'Graph Causal Optimal Transport \ and Wasserstein Distances'] Let $G$ be a directed graph, $\mu \in \probabilities{\mathcal{X}}$, $
u \in \probabilities{\mathcal{Y}}$. We call a coupling $\pi \in \couplings{\mu}{
u}$ $G$-causal if there exists a pair of random variables $(X,Y) \sim \pi$, measurable functions
    $$f_i \colon \mathcal{X}_i 	imes \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{pa}_{i}} 	imes \mathcal{Y}_{\mathrm{pa}_{i}} 	imes \mathbb{R} 	o \mathcal{Y}_i, \quad i\in V,$$
    and $\mathbb{R}$-valued random variables $(U_i)_{i\in V}$ such that $\sigma(X), (\sigma(U_i))_{i \in V}$ are independent and
    $$Y_i = f_i(X_i, X_{\mathrm{pa}_{i}}, Y_{\mathrm{pa}_{i}}, U_i), \quad  orall \, i \in V.$$
    We denote by $\Gcouplings{\mu}{
u}$ the set of all such $G$-causal couplings. Moreover, we define the set of $G$-anticausal couplings $\Ganticouplings{\mu}{
u}$ to be the set of measures in $\couplings{\mu}{
u}$ whose pushforward under the map $(x,y) \mapsto (y,x)$ is in $\Gcouplings{
u}{\mu}$, and the set of $G$-bicausal couplings $\Gbicouplings{\mu}{
u} \coloneqq \Gcouplings{\mu}{
u} \cap \Ganticouplings{\mu}{
u}$.

