[Lemma S32L of arXiv:2410.23899, 'Tightness for interlacing geometric random walk bridges'] Fix $p, M\in (0, \infty)$. There exists $N_2 \in \mathbb{N}$, depending on $p$ and $M$, such that the following holds. If $M_1, M_2 \in \mathbb{R}$ are such that $|M_1 - M_2| \leq M$, $n \geq N_2$, $x, y \in \mathbb{Z}$ are such that $x \leq y$ and $x \geq M_1 n^{1/2}$, $y \geq pn + M_2 n^{1/2}$, $g: \llbracket 0, n \rrbracket \rightarrow [-\infty, \infty)$ is arbitrary such that $\Omega_{\mathsf{Inter}}(0,n,x,y, \infty, g) \neq \emptyset$, and $s \in [0, n]$, then we have
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}_{\mathsf{Inter}, \operatorname{Geom}}^{0,n, x, y, \infty, g} \left( Q_1(s) \geq \frac{n - s}{n} \cdot M_1 n^{1/2} + \frac{s}{n} \cdot (pn + M_2 n^{1/2}) - 3n^{1/4} \right) \geq \frac{1}{3}.
\end{equation}

