[Proposition 12 (Degeneracy of $\dist_{\mathrm{AW}}$) of arXiv:2604.22159, 'Adapted optimal transport between filtered Gaussian processes'] For $L, M \in \mathscr{L}(N, d)$ the following are equivalent:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $\dist_{\mathrm{AW}}(L, M) = 0$, that is, $L \sim M$.
\item[(ii)] There exists $Q = \diag(Q_1, \ldots, Q_N)$, with $Q_t \in \mathscr{O}(d)$ for all $t \in [N]$, such that
\begin{equation} 
M = LQ.
\end{equation}
That is, $M_{s,t} = L_{s,t}Q_t$ (or $L_{s,t} = M_{s,t} Q_t^{\intercal}$) for $s, t \in [N]$. We denote by $\mathscr{O}(N, d) \cong \prod_{t = 1}^N \mathscr{O}(d)$  the set of $Nd \times Nd$ block diagonal matrices whose diagonal blocks are orthogonal.
\end{itemize}
In particular, we have 
\begin{equation} 
\dist_{\AW}(L, M) = 0 \Rightarrow LL^{\intercal} = MM^{\intercal}
\end{equation}
(that is, the covariance matrices coincide) and
\begin{equation} 
\dist_{\AW}(L, M) = \dist_{\AW}(L, MQ)
\end{equation}
for $L, M \in \mathscr{L}(N, d)$ and $Q \in \mathscr{O}(N, d)$.

