{"artifact_digest":"sha256:6a7a7528d09cf625c088984d8920459e930cc14464225c086035ccbf56fffd0e","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/6a/6a7a7528d09cf625c088984d8920459e930cc14464225c086035ccbf56fffd0e.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:367786b19d1c0d2915309aa70691f784a40e484af93365b0b3036560643e0bc8","bundle_entry_key":"2603-27144-lemma-34-green-positivity","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_102","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_102.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2603-27144-lem-34-green-positivity","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: \begin{comment}
Alternative proof:
\begin{proof}
Let $P$ be the transition matrix for the simple random on $G$.Then $(g_{u,w})_{u,w\in\mathcal{E}}$ is a positive semi-definite matrix since
\[
g_{u,w}=\sum_{n=0}^{\left\lfloor \frac{M_{\mathrm{LE}}-1}{2}\right\rfloor }P^{2n}(u,w)
\]
and $P$ is symmetric. The same holds with $\mathcal{O}$ instead of $\mathcal{E}$. Observe that for each $n\ge0$
\begin{align*}
