body:
[Proposition 11 of arXiv:2604.22567, 'Sign-balance of random Laplace eigenfunctions'] For every $r>1$ there exists a sequence of smooth functions $f_{j}:\R^{2}\rightarrow\R$ with the following properties as $j \to \infty$:
\begin{enumerate}[i.]

\item Uniformly over $x$ in compact subsets of $\R^2$,
\begin{equation}

\frac{1}{\pi}\int\limits_{B_{1}(x)} H(f_{j}(y))dy \rightarrow 0.
\end{equation}

\item There exists a number $z=z(r) > 0$ so that 
\begin{equation}

\frac{1}{\pi r^{2}}\int\limits_{B_{r}(0)} H(f_{j}(y))dy \rightarrow z.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

