body:
[Def bousfield-lattice of arXiv:2608.26876, 'Local Bousfield classes via homological support'] Let $\cat T$ be a well generated tensor-triangulated category. The set of homological Bousfield classes $\BL(\cat T)$ is a complete lattice when ordered by reverse inclusion:
	\[
	  \mathinner{\left\langle A \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}\le \mathinner{\left\langle B \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}\ \Longleftrightarrow\ \mathinner{\left\langle A \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}\supseteq \mathinner{\left\langle B \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}.
	\]
	The top and bottom elements are $\mathinner{\left\langle \unit \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}=\{0\}$ and $\mathinner{\left\langle 0 \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}=\cat T$ respectively. The join is given by
	\begin{equation}
		\bigvee\SETT{\mathinner{\left\langle A_i \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}}{i \in I} = 
		\big\langle\hspace{-0.2ex}
		\coprod\nolimits_{i \in I} \hspace{-0.2ex}A_i \big\rangle_*
	\end{equation}
	and the meet is given by 
	\begin{equation}
		\bigwedge\SETT{\mathinner{\left\langle A_i \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}}{i\in I} = \bigvee\SETT{\mathinner{\left\langle X \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}}{\mathinner{\left\langle X \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *} \le \mathinner{\left\langle A_i \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *} \text{ for all } i \in I}.
	\end{equation}
	In general, the meet operation $\mathinner{\left\langle A \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *} \meet \mathinner{\left\langle B \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}$ is different from $\mathinner{\left\langle A \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *} \otimes \mathinner{\left\langle B \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *} \coloneqq \mathinner{\left\langle A \otimes B \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}$ although we always have $\mathinner{\left\langle A \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *} \otimes \mathinner{\left\langle B \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}\leq \mathinner{\left\langle A \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *} \meet \mathinner{\left\langle B \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}$. For example, the Brown--Comenetz dual of the sphere $I\in {\mathscr{S}p}$ satisfies $I \otimes I = 0$; see [HoveyPalmieri99]. Hence $\mathinner{\left\langle I \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}\otimes \mathinner{\left\langle I \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *} = \mathinner{\left\langle 0 \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}$ but $\mathinner{\left\langle I \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *} \meet \mathinner{\left\langle I \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}=\mathinner{\left\langle I \right\rangle}_{\mkern-2mu *}$ in $\BL({\mathscr{S}p})$.

[Proposition 11 of arXiv:2606.20908, 'On It\^o-Stratonovich formula for rough sheets'] Let $g\in\cp_{2,2}$ satisfying the following assumptions:
\begin{equation*}
\doo g \in \cp_{3,2}^{\ga_1,*}, \qquad
\dt g \in \cp_{2,3}^{*,\ga_2}, \qquad
\der g \in \cp_{3,3}^{\ga_1,\ga_2},
\end{equation*}
for $\ga_1,\ga_2>1$, where the spaces $\cp$ are introduced in Definition~\ref{def:2D-hnorm-P2P3} and we recall that $*$ denotes any kind of H\"older regularity. Then there exists $f\in\cp^{1,1}$ such that
\begin{equation*}
\der f = \lc  \id - \laa_1 \doo \rc \lc  \id - \laa_2 \dt \rc g,
\quad\mbox{and}\quad
\lim_{|\pi|\to 0}\sum_{\si_i,\tau_j\in\pi} g_{\si_{i} \si_{i+1} ;  \tau_{j} \tau_{j+1}} = \der f_{s_1 s_2; t_1 t_2},
\end{equation*}
where $\pi$ designates a family of rectangular partitions of $[s_1,s_2]\times[t_1,t_2]$ whose mesh goes to 0. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma pmf of x+y of arXiv:2603.09008, 'On the statistics of random-to-top shuffles'] Let $X\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(a)$ and let $Y$ be geometric with $P(Y=k)=a(1-a)^k$. Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are independent. Then, we have 
    \begin{equation}

        \mathbb{P}(X+Y=\ell)=\sum_{j=0}^{\ell}\frac{a(1-a)^j e^{-a}a^{\ell -j}}{(\ell -j)!}
    \end{equation}

[Theorem thm:intro-lcp-spectrum of arXiv:2608.02459, 'Response Calculus for Spectral Simplicity\\ and Joint Eigenvalue Densities'] For every $0<\gamma<2$, almost surely, $A_h^{\rm C}$ has compact resolvent, its
kernel consists exactly of the constants, and, with the positive eigenvalues
listed with multiplicity,
\begin{equation}
 0=\Lambda_0^{{\rm C},h}
 <\Lambda_1^{{\rm C},h}<\Lambda_2^{{\rm C},h}<\cdots\uparrow\infty.

\end{equation}
Moreover, for every $N\ge1$ and $1\le n_1<\cdots<n_N$,
\begin{equation}
 (\Lambda_{n_1}^{{\rm C},h},\ldots,
  \Lambda_{n_N}^{{\rm C},h})_\#\mathbb P_{\rm C}
 \ll\mathcal L^N.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition a18 of arXiv:2411.19926, 'Sparse Pseudospectral Shattering'] Let $A$ be any random matrix. Fix any $\tau_0 > 0$ and $R > \eta_0 > 0$. Let $\varepsilon = \eta_0/\pare{n\tau_0^{1/2}}$.
Then
\spliteq{}{
\Pr\mleft(\max_j\kappa(\lambda_j)^2\le\tau_0\mright)
\ge
\Pr\mleft(\eta(A)\ge\eta_0\mright)
-\sqbrac{3n\lg\frac{3R}{\eta_0} + 3\lg\frac{2\eta_0}{\tau_0}}
\cdot\frac{\E\vol\Lambda_\varepsilon(A)}{\tau_0\cdot\varepsilon^2}
-\Pr\mleft(\left\|A\right\|>R\mright).}

