[Prop bdy-sin of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] Let $\alpha\in(0,1]$. Let $D$ be a bounded, simply connected domain. Fix any $A\subseteq \partial D$ to be a (deterministic) closed subset of the boundary such that $\mathrm{Leb}(A)=0$. For any continuous, bounded  function $f:\mathbb C\to\mathbb R$,
\begin{align}
    \lim_{n\to \infty} (:\! \sin(\alpha \phi) \!: , f\1_{[A]_n}) = 0, 
\end{align}
where the limit is almost sure and in $L^2(\mathbb P)$.
In particular, for any $u\in[-\pi/2,\pi/2]$,
\begin{align}
    \lim_{n\to \infty} (\field{\overline{\mathrm{cos}}(\alpha\phi+u)}, f\1_{[A]_n}) = 0,
\end{align}
where the limit is, again, almost sure and in $L^2(\mathbb P)$.

