body:
[Theorem Main1 of arXiv:2603.14302, 'On Partition Functions for Time-Inhomogeneous Branching Random Walks'] \textsc{(Phase Transition for Time-homogeneous BRWs)}
For a BRW on a supercritical Galton–Watson tree with mean offspring number $\mathbb{E}[D_1] = d > 1$, there exists a phase transition for the non-degeneracy of $W_\infty^\beta$, i.e.,
\[
\beta_c := \sup\Bigl\{\beta \geq 0 \mid \mathbb{P}\{W_\infty^\beta = 0\} > 0\Bigr\}
\]
is well-defined and equals $\sqrt{2\log d}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem lemma:many_to_two of arXiv:2509.06730, 'Hyperbolic branching Brownian motion: \\ the empirical limit measure'] {Fix $r\geq 0$,} under $\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}^r$, let $(X_s^1,Y_s^1)_{s\geq 0}$ and $(X_s^2,Y_s^2)_{s\geq 0}$ be two hyperbolic Brownian motions that move together until time $r$ and afterwards move independently. Then we have for any interval $I\subseteq \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ that 
    \begin{equation*}
        \ensuremath{\mathbb{E}}\left[\mu_\infty(I)^2\right] = 2\beta\int_0^\infty\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}^r\left(X^1_\infty,X_\infty^2\in I\right) e^{-\beta r}dr.
    \end{equation*}
    Similarly for any $K>0$,
        \begin{align*}
        &\ensuremath{\mathbb{E}}\left[\mu_\infty^K(I)^2\right] = 2\beta\int_0^\infty\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}^r\bigg(X^1_\infty,X_\infty^2\in I, \\ 
        & \hspace{4cm}\forall s\geq K: \log(Y_s^1)+s/2, \log(Y_s^2)+s/2 \in [-s^{2/3},s^{2/3}]\bigg) e^{-\beta r}dr.
    \end{align*}

