body:
[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2607.23305, 'On extremes of a random walk with positive drift\\ over an intermediate regularly varying '] Assume that $a:=\E\xi>0$ and that the distribution of $\tau$ is intermediate regularly varying at infinity.
If
\begin{eqnarray}
x \P\{\xi>x\} &=& o(\P\{\tau>x\})\quad\mbox{as }x\to\infty,
\end{eqnarray}
then 
\begin{eqnarray}
\P\{M_\tau>x\} &\le& (1+o(1))\P\{\tau>x/a\}\quad\mbox{as  } x\to\infty.
\end{eqnarray}
If
\begin{eqnarray}
\int_x^\infty \P\{\xi<-y\}dy &=& o(\P\{\tau>x\})\quad\mbox{as }x\to\infty,
\end{eqnarray}
then 
\begin{eqnarray}
\P\{S_\tau>x\} &\ge& (1+o(1))\P\{\tau>x/a\}\quad\mbox{as  } x\to\infty.
\end{eqnarray}
If both conditions, \eqref{F.tau.cond.sstar.upper} and \eqref{F.tau.cond.sstar.lower}, hold true then 
\begin{eqnarray}
\P\{M_\tau>x\}\ \sim\ \P\{S_\tau>x\} &\sim& \P\{\tau>x/a\}\quad\mbox{as  } x\to\infty.
\end{eqnarray} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 34 (Multiplication between the $n$th and first chaos) of arXiv:2605.14630, '2605.14630'] Assume $f\in\H^{\otimes n}$ and $g\in\H$. Then 
\begin{equation}
 \hat I_n(f)\hat I_1(g) 
 = \hat I_{n+1}(f\otimes g) + \hat I_{n-1}(f\star_1g)\;,
\end{equation} 
where $\star_1$ denotes the \emph{contraction operation} 
\begin{equation}

 (f\star_1g)(i_1,\dots,i_{n-1}) 
 = \sum_{\Sigma\in\frakS(1,n)}
 \sum_{j=1}^N f(\Sigma(j,i_1,\dots,i_{n-1}))g(j)\;. 
\end{equation}

