body:
[Definition Invariant sets and ergodicity of arXiv:2606.13615, 'Data-Driven Subsampling Rates for Diffusion Parameter Estimation of SDEs'] Let $\mathbf{Z}=(\mathbf{Z}_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}_0}$ be a stationary $\mathbb{R}^d$-valued process.
A set $A \in \mathcal{A}$ is called invariant w.r.t. $\mathbf{Z}$ if there exists some $B \in \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^d)^{\mathbb{N}_0}$ such that
\begin{equation*}
A = \lbrace (\mathbf{Z}_j, \mathbf{Z}_{j+1}, \ldots) \in B \rbrace
\end{equation*}
is true for all $j \in \mathbb{N}_0$. Furthermore, $\mathbf{Z}$ is called ergodic if any invariant set $A \in \mathcal{A}$ satisfies $P(A) \in \lbrace 0,1 \rbrace$.

