body:
[Definition eq:sub-Laplacian of arXiv:2609.04045, 'Heat content and spectrum for subordinated sub-Laplacians'] Let $\{X_1,\ldots,X_{m}\}$ be an orthonormal frame for $\mathcal{D}$. Define the \emph{horizontal gradient} of $f$, denoted by
$\nabla_{\mathcal{H}}f$, as the horizontal section
\begin{equation*}
\nabla_{\mathcal{H}}f:=\sum_{i=1}^{m}(X_if)X_i,
\end{equation*}
for any function $f:{\mathbb G}\longrightarrow {\mathbb R}$ for which the partial derivatives
$X_if$ exist. The second-order differential operator 
\begin{equation}
\Delta_{\mathcal{H}}:=-\sum_{i=1}^{m} X_i^2
\end{equation}
defined on $C^{\infty}(\mathbb{G})$ is called a \emph{sub-Laplacian}.

