body:
[Proposition prop:frac_per_bound of arXiv:2609.04045, 'Heat content and spectrum for subordinated sub-Laplacians'] Let $\Omega$ be any measurable subset of $\mathbb{G}$. Then for all $0<\alpha<1$,
    \begin{align}
        \operatorname{Per}^{(\alpha)}_{\mathcal H}(\Omega)\leqslant C_\alpha \operatorname{Vol}(\Omega)^{1-\alpha} \operatorname{Per}_{\mathcal H}(\Omega)^\alpha
    \end{align}
    with 
    \begin{align*}
        C_\alpha=\frac{\left(2Q\right)^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}}{(1-\alpha)\Gamma(1-\frac{\alpha}{2})} ,
    \end{align*}
    where $Q$ is the homogeneous dimension of $\mathbb G$, and $m$ is the dimension of the first layer of the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$.
     As a result, $\operatorname{Per}^{(\alpha)}_{\mathcal H}(\Omega)<\infty$ for all $\alpha\in (0,1)$ whenever $\operatorname{Vol}(\Omega)<\infty$ and $\operatorname{Per}_{\mathcal H}(\Omega)<\infty$. In particular, when $\Omega$ is bounded with a $C^1$ boundary, $\operatorname{Per}^{(\alpha)}_{\mathcal H}(\Omega)<\infty$ for all $\alpha\in (0,1)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop extinction-explosion of arXiv:2411.09629, 'A Kesten Stigum theorem for Galton-Watson processes with infinitely many types in a random'] Let $(Z_n)$ be a MGWRE. We assume that (ass: boundedness_KS of the paper)-(ass: LlogL of the paper) hold, that the environmental sequence ${\bar{\xi}}$ is i.i.d and that there exists a type $x_0\in\mathbb{X}$ such that ${\mathbb P}$-a.s, conditionally on the survival event, for any initial population $Z_0$,
	\begin{equation} \limsup_{n\rightarrow \infty} Z_n(\{x_0\})=+\infty.\end{equation}
	Then, for any initial population $Z_0$, the events $\ext=\{\exists n\geq 0, Z_n=0\}$ and $\{W=0\}$ coincide up to a ${\mathbb P}$-negligible event. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Defi def:cpls of arXiv:2509.08659, 'Gap metrics for stationary point processes and quantitative convexity of the free energy'] For two stationary probability measures $\Pi_i\in \mathcal P_{s,1}( \mathsf{S})$, $i=0,1$ we denote by $ \mathsf{Cpl}_s(\Pi_0,\Pi_1)$ the set of all couplings of $\Pi_0$ and $\Pi_1$, which are invariant under the diagonal shifts 
    \begin{align*}
         \mathsf{S}^2 \ni ( \mathsf{s}, \mathsf{s}')\mapsto (\sigma^ks,\sigma^ks')\in 
         \mathsf{S}^2,\quad k\in \IZ.
    \end{align*}

