[Definition px of arXiv:2411.15881, 'Stable Approximation for Call Function Via Stein's method'] If $X$ has a distribution function of the form
\begin{equation}
F_{X}(x)=\big(1-\frac{A+B(x)}{|x|^{\alpha}}(1+\delta)\big){\bf 1}_{[0,\infty)}(x)+\frac{A+B(x)}{|x|^{\alpha}}(1-\delta){\bf 1}_{(-\infty,0)}(x)\big),
\end{equation}
where $\alpha\in(1,2)$, $A>0$, $\delta\in[-1,1]$ and  $B: \mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ is a bounded function vanishing at $\pm\infty$, then we say that $X$ is in the domain $\mathcal{R}_\alpha$ of normal attraction of a stable law of exponent $\alpha$.

[Lemma Vtozfour of arXiv:2511.06321, 'Torus scaling limits and the plateau of the critical weakly coupled $|\varphi|^4$ model in'] For $f \in C^{\infty} (\T^d)$ and $V_{N} \in \tilde\cD_{N,\varnothing}$,
\begin{align}
	\lim_{N\rightarrow \infty} I_{N}^{\Lambda} (v_N ) = \lim_{N\rightarrow \infty} e^{-V_{N}^{{(\textbf{s})}} (\Lambda,  v_N ) } = e^{-\frac{1}{4} |z|^4}

\end{align}
and they are uniformly integrable in $z$.

