[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2604.27485, 'On large deviation principles for general random processes \footnote{Research supported by'] In the general case, the l.d.p.\ always implies the l.l.d.p. Con\-versely, let the following condition be met: for any $N>0$ there exists a $v>0$ such that 
\begin{equation}

\limsup_{T\to\infty} \frac1T \ln \mathbf{P} (|z(T)|>v) \le -N.
\end{equation}
Then the  l.l.d.p.\ implies the l.d.p. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary 18 (Support and edge regularity) of arXiv:2608.26693, '2608.26693'] For $c\in(0,1)$ and $r>0$, the factor law $\rho_{c,r}$ is a compactly supported Jacobi measure on $(0,1)$ with exponent $1/2$ at both endpoints.  Its free multiplicative square has a single support interval: there exist $0<E_-(c,r)<E_+(c,r)<1$ such that
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{supp}(\rho_{c,r}\boxtimes\rho_{c,r})=[E_-(c,r),E_+(c,r)],
\end{equation}
and the product density has square-root decay at both $E_-(c,r)$ and $E_+(c,r)$.

