[Definition 9 of arXiv:2606.00945, 'Ising model and percolation: from hexagonal lattice to 3-12 lattice'] For a general polygon $(\Omega;y_1,\ldots,y_{2N})$, the pure partition functions for multiple $\SLE_{\kappa}$ are defined as 
\begin{align*}
    \LZ_{\alpha}^{(\kappa)}(\Omega;y_1,\ldots,y_{2N}):=\prod_{i=1}^{2N}|\varphi'(y_i)|^h\times \LZ_{\alpha}^{(\kappa)}(\varphi(y_1),\ldots,\varphi(y_{2N})), \qquad\text{for }\alpha\in\LP_N, 
\end{align*}
where $\varphi:\Omega\to \HH$ is a conformal mapping such that $\varphi(y_1)<\cdots<\varphi(y_{2N})$. By M\"obius covariance~\eqref{eqn::COV}, this definition is independent of the choice of $\varphi$.

[Theorem thm-5 of arXiv:2412.08964, 'Phase transition and critical behavior in hierarchical\\integer-valued Gaussian and Coulom'] Suppose Assumption~(ass-1 of the paper) holds. For each~$b\ge2$ there exists~$\epsilon>0$ and, for all~$\beta>0$ with~$1<b\theta<1+\epsilon$, there exist $\eta>0$, $C>0$ and a sequence $\{\lambda_\star(q)\}_{q\in\mathbb Z}$ with $\lambda_\star(0)=1$, $\lambda_\star(q)\le\theta^{q^2}$ for~$q\in\mathbb Z$ and
\begin{equation}

0<\lambda_\star(q)=\lambda_\star(-q)\le \bigl(2b^{1/2}\sqrt{b\theta-1}\,\bigr)^{|q|},\quad q\in\mathbb Z,
\end{equation}
for which the following is true: For all initial~$\nu$ subject to Assumption~(ass-2 of the paper) there exists~$k_0\ge0$ such that for all integer $n$ and~$k$ with $\min\{k,n-k\}\ge k_0$ and all~$q\in\mathbb Z$,
\begin{equation}

a_k(q)\le a_k(0) \bigl(2b^{1/2}\sqrt{b\theta-1}\,\bigr)^{|q|}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}

\biggl|\frac{a_k(q)}{a_k(0)}-\lambda_\star(q)\biggr|\le C^{|q|}\biggl[\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{-\eta k}+\sum_{j=0}^k\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{-\eta(k-j)}\,\fraktura d_{\min\{j,n-j\}}\biggr]
\end{equation}
 hold  with~$\{\fraktura d_j\}_{j\ge0}$ denoting the sequence from Assumption~(ass-1 of the paper). Moreover, each $v_k$ is a $C^\infty$ function with   with~$\{v_k'\}_{k\ge0}$ and~$\{v_k''\}_{k\ge0}$ uniformly bounded and, defining $v_\star\colon\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ by 
\begin{equation}

\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{-v_\star(z)}:=\Biggl(\,\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}
q_1,\dots,q_b\in\mathbb Z\\q_1+\dots+q_b=0
\end{subarray}}
\prod_{i=1}^b \lambda_\star(q_i)\Biggr)^{-\frac1{b-1}}
\sum_{q\in\mathbb Z}\lambda_\star(q)\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{2\pi\text{\rm  i}\mkern0.7mu q z},
\end{equation}
we have  $v_k(z)-b v_{k-1}(z')\to v_\star(z)-bv_\star(z')$ and $v_k'(z)\to v'_\star(z)$ as~$\min\{k,n-k\}\to\infty$, uniformly in $z,z'\in\mathbb R$.  (The existence of~$v_\star'$ is ensured by (equation E:3.28u of the paper).) In addition, assuming $\{\fraktura d_k\}_{k\ge0}$ decays exponentially fast, there exist  $\eta'>0$ and~$C'>0$ such that
\begin{equation}

\sup_{z,z'\in\mathbb R}\,\Bigl| v_{k+1}(z)-b v_{k}(z')- \bigl[v_\star(z)-bv_\star(z')\bigr]\Bigr|\le C'\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{-\eta'\min\{k,n-k\}}
\end{equation}
 for all $n > k \ge 0$ and 
\begin{equation}

\sup_{z\in\mathbb R}\,\bigl| v_k'(z)-v'_\star(z)\bigr|\le C'\text{\rm  e}\mkern0.7mu^{-\eta'\min\{k,n-k\}}
\end{equation}
 for all~$n\ge k\ge0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:Push-TASEP-WF of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] The product-graph wave functions satisfy the
semi-discrete linear problem
(equation eq:sc-linear-1 of the paper)--(equation eq:sc-linear-j of the paper)
and its adjoint
(equation eq:sc-adjoint-1 of the paper)--(equation eq:sc-adjoint-j of the paper)
with the shifts and edge weights above.
Explicitly:
\begin{align}
\partial_t \Psi^{t,\mathbf{a},\mathbf{n}}
&= 2\bigl(\Psi^{t,\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{1},\mathbf{n}}
   - \Psi^{t,\mathbf{a},\mathbf{n}}\bigr),
 \\
\Psi^{t,\mathbf{a},\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{1}}
&= 2\Psi^{t,\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{1},\mathbf{n}}
   - \Psi^{t,\mathbf{a},\mathbf{n}},

\end{align}
and
\begin{align}
\partial_t \Phi^{t,\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{1},\mathbf{n}}
&= 2\bigl(\Phi^{t,\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{1},\mathbf{n}}
   - \Phi^{t,\mathbf{a},\mathbf{n}}\bigr),
 \\
\Phi^{t,\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{1},\mathbf{n}}
&= 2\Phi^{t,\mathbf{a},\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{1}}
   - \Phi^{t,\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{1},\mathbf{n}+\mathbf{1}}.

\end{align}

