[Definition def:central_region of arXiv:2609.01697, 'Unique ergodicity of projective cocycles over the 2D stochastic Navier--Stokes equations'] For a fixed parameter $R > 0$, the {\em central region} $\mathcal{C}_R$ is the sublevel set
  \begin{equation}
    \mathcal{C}_R \overset{\mathrm{\scriptscriptstyle def}}{=} \left\{ z = (w, \pi) \in \mathcal{X} : \|w\|_{H^{s_\star}} + \|\pi\|_{H^1} \leqslant R \right\} .
  \end{equation}

Let $(S, d)$ be a metric space with its Borel $\sigma$-algebra, and let $\mu_n$ ($n \ge 1$) and $\mu$ be Borel PROBABILITY measures on $S$. The following are equivalent:
  (a) $\mu_n \Rightarrow \mu$, i.e. $\int f \, d\mu_n \to \int f \, d\mu$ for every bounded continuous $f: S \to \mathbb{R}$;
  (b) $\int f \, d\mu_n \to \int f \, d\mu$ for every bounded uniformly continuous $f$ (it suffices to take bounded Lipschitz $f$);
  (c) $\limsup_n \mu_n(F) \le \mu(F)$ for every closed set $F \subset S$;
  (d) $\liminf_n \mu_n(G) \ge \mu(G)$ for every open set $G \subset S$;
  (e) $\mu_n(A) \to \mu(A)$ for every Borel set $A$ with $\mu(\partial A) = 0$ (a $\mu$-continuity set), where $\partial A = \overline{A} \setminus \mathrm{int}(A)$;
  (f) $\int f \, d\mu_n \to \int f \, d\mu$ for every bounded Borel function $f$ whose set of discontinuity points $D_f$ satisfies $\mu(D_f) = 0$.
The same equivalences hold on $S = \mathbb{R}$ with (e) restricted to half-lines: $\mu_n \Rightarrow \mu$ iff the distribution functions converge, $F_n(x) \to F(x)$, at every continuity point $x$ of $F$.

Sharp edges. (1) The equal total mass (probability measures, or at least finite measures with $\mu_n(S) \to \mu(S)$) is essential: for finite measures with $\mu_n(S) \not\to \mu(S)$, (c) alone or (d) alone does not give (a); e.g. $\mu_n = 0$ satisfies (c) against any $\mu$. Passing between (c) and (d) uses complements together with $\mu_n(S) = \mu(S) = 1$. (2) In (e) the boundary condition is on the LIMIT measure $\mu$, not on the $\mu_n$: $\delta_{1/n} \Rightarrow \delta_0$ but $\delta_{1/n}((0, \infty)) = 1 \not\to 0 = \delta_0((0,\infty))$ because $\delta_0(\partial (0,\infty)) = \delta_0(\{0\}) = 1$. (3) Bounded test functions only: $\mu_n = (1-1/n)\delta_0 + (1/n)\delta_n \Rightarrow \delta_0$ on $\mathbb{R}$ yet $\int x \, d\mu_n = 1 \not\to 0$; nothing is asserted about moments without uniform integrability. (4) The inequalities in (c) and (d) can be strict: $\delta_{1/n} \Rightarrow \delta_0$ with $F = \{0\}$ gives $\limsup \mu_n(F) = 0 < 1 = \mu(F)$.
(S, d) is a metric space with its Borel sigma-algebra
mu_n and mu are Borel probability measures (equal total mass 1 is used in passing between closed-set and open-set forms)
test functions in (a),(b) are bounded; boundary-null condition in (e) is with respect to the limit measure mu

