body:
[Definition 3 (Bicausal coupling) of arXiv:2604.22159, 'Adapted optimal transport between filtered Gaussian processes'] Let $\bX$ and $\bY$ be filtered processes. A coupling between $\bX$ and $\bY$ is a probability measure $\pi$ on $(\Omega^{\bX} \times \Omega^{\bY}, \cF^{\bX} \otimes \cF^{\bY})$ whose marginals are $\bP^{\bX}$ and $\bP^{\bY}$. We say that $\pi$ is bicausal if for every $1 \leq t \leq N$ we have, under $\pi$,
\begin{equation} 
\begin{split}
&\cF_{N, 0}^{\bX, \bY} \indep \cF_{0,t}^{\bX, \bY} \text{ given } \cF_{t,0}^{\bX, \bY}, \text{ and } \cF_{0, N}^{\bX, \bY} \indep \cF_{t,0}^{\bX, \bY} \text{ given } \cF_{0,t}^{\bX, \bY}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
We let $\Cpl_{\mathrm{bc}}(\bX, \bY)$ be the set of bicausal couplings between $\bX$ and $\bY$. When $\bX = \bX^{\mu}$ and $\bY = \bX^{\nu}$ are canonical representations of $\mu, \nu \in \cP(\bR^{Nd})$, we write $\Cpl_{\mathrm{bc}}(\mu, \nu) := \Cpl_{\mathrm{bc}}(\bX^{\mu}, \bX^{\nu})$.

[Corollary cor:state-indep-diffusion of arXiv:2608.21805, 'Logarithmic derivatives of variational and singular stochastic partial differential equati'] For SPDEs with state-independent diffusion $\mathcal{B}(t, u) = \mathcal{B}(t)$ (independent of $u$) but nonlinear drift $\mathcal{A}(t,u)$, we have $\mathcal{B}'_u = 0$ and $\mathcal{B}''_{uu} = 0$. The Malliavin--second variation equation (equation eq:second-var of the paper) simplifies to
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  \mathrm{d}\mathcal{Z}(\tau,s;r) + \mathcal{A}'_u(\tau,X(\tau))\mathcal{Z}(\tau,s;r)\,\mathrm{d}\tau
  &= -\mathcal{A}''_{uu}(\tau,X(\tau))(\mathcal{D}_r X(\tau),\,Y(\tau,s))\,\mathrm{d}\tau, \\
  \mathcal{Z}(\max(s,r),s;r) &= 0,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
(a random PDE with no stochastic integral term, but with random coefficients depending on the SPDE solution). The Malliavin derivative $\mathcal{D}_r\Phi_s$ of the covering integrand $\Phi_s = Y(t,s)\mathcal{B}(s)$ reduces to $\mathcal{D}_r\Phi_s = \mathcal{Z}(t,s;r)\mathcal{B}(s)$ in both cases of Proposition (prop:D-gamma of the paper), and the Bismut formula (equation eq:skorokhod-decomp of the paper) involves only the first variation $Y$, the second variation $\mathcal{Z}$, and the operator derivatives $\mathcal{A}'_u$, $\mathcal{A}''_{uu}$.

