 \mathbf{P}_{\epsilon}(\underline{y}\overset{\lambda}\rightarrow\underline{z})\leq c_n\frac{1}{[\epsilon^{-2}\lambda]^{n/2}+1}
 \]
To finish the proof it is enough to note that 
if $\epsilon^{-2}\lambda\geq 1$, since $n\geq 2$ then  $(\epsilon^{-2}\lambda)^{\frac{n}{2}}+1\geq (\epsilon^{-2}\lambda)^{\frac{n}{2}}\geq  (\epsilon^{-2}\lambda)^{1-\zeta}$ for some $\zeta>0$. From this  the ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:70ced66f42517264c9b19aa6f69ef8a9ed6e75a598a1471dfa7a1aae7f16440d","revision_digest":"sha256:d42e3061c05b058e90ea4ecf2cfad49994c161463eda3c134b105567cb852a05","source_id":"arxiv:2512.12902","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

