[Corollary 9 (Best Euclidean pole) of arXiv:2609.02595, 'Minimal Radial Sub-Gamma Envelopes for Infinitely Divisible Random Vectors'] Let
\begin{equation}
 \overline C_X=\sup_{\|u\|=1}C_X(u).
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 K_X(t)\leq\frac{t^\top\Sigma t}{2(1-\overline C_X\|t\|)},
 \qquad \overline C_X\|t\|<1.
\end{equation}
Moreover, \(\overline C_X\) is the smallest constant that can replace it in \eqref{eq:covariance-isotropic}.

[Theorem 21 (Critical residual geometry) of arXiv:2608.26877, 'When Is the Sharp Covariance Envelope Tight? Feature-Only Geometry for Volume-Sampled Leas'] Let $d\ge2$ and let $A\in\R^{2d\times d}$ have orthonormal columns and
$\lVert a_i\rVert_2^2=1/2$ for every $i$.  Then
\begin{equation}
  \boxed{
  \frac{1-\rho_A}{4}
  \le \nu_A
  \le \frac{(1-\rho_A)(3+\rho_A)}{8}.}

\end{equation}
Moreover, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=*]
\item $\nu_A=0$;
\item there are distinct indices $i,j$, a unit vector $v\in\R^d$, and
signs $\sigma_i,\sigma_j\in\{\pm1\}$ such that
\begin{equation}
  a_i=\frac{\sigma_i}{\sqrt2}v,
  \qquad
  a_j=\frac{\sigma_j}{\sqrt2}v,
  \qquad
  a_k^\top v=0\quad(k\notin\{i,j\}).

\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Hence the zero boundary is the projectively repeated-pair boundary with an
orthogonal remainder, while \eqref{eq:critical-coherence-sandwich} controls
the magnitude of the continuous residual-direction margin away from that
boundary. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

