[Proposition p:bifur dual of arXiv:2411.04773, 'Meeting of squared Bessel flow lines and application to the skew Brownian motion'] Almost surely, for any $a>0$, $b\ge 0$ and $r<x$,
\begin{equation}
   (a,r) \textrm{ is an ancestor of } (b,x) \Leftrightarrow   a=\mathcal S^*_{-x,-r}(b). 
\end{equation}

[Lem sigma_l of arXiv:2509.26254, '$K$-Branching Random Walk with Noisy Selection: \\ \Large Large Population Limits and Phas'] Recall $\bm{u}$ from (equation eq:defDelta of the paper) and define the piecewise linear operator $\Sigma : \mathbb{R}^k \to \mathbb{R}$ by
\begin{equation}
\Sigma(\bm{v}) := \begin{cases}
\left\langle \bm{u},\bm{v} \right\rangle - \gamma - \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} v_i - \frac{1-k\beta}{1\wedge\beta} v_{k} + \frac{1 - \gamma}{1\wedge\beta} & \text{ if } \ \  v_{k} < \frac{1-\gamma}{1-k\beta}, \\ \\
\left\langle \bm{u},\bm{v} \right\rangle - \gamma - \sum_{i=1}^{k} v_i + \frac{1 - \gamma}{1-k\beta} & \text{ otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
For all $g \in \mathcal{T}$,
\begin{equation}

\sigma^*(r(g))  = U(g) + \Sigma(\bm{v}(g)). 
\end{equation}

