{"artifact_digest":"sha256:a4b4050fcc42d7e2c7a43adcf607272ed34d4f1a553128a1405e09229bbd0208","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/a4/a4b4050fcc42d7e2c7a43adcf607272ed34d4f1a553128a1405e09229bbd0208.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:e4fd5714f969a559a31b7b3fc3b423e4ab80d3f027124b1676015e9ba07ab99c","bundle_entry_key":"2607-27150-lemma-5-cite-lemma-3-9","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_081","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_081.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-27150-lemma-5-cite-lemma-3-9","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Since both $A(L(t))$ and $A(L(t)-)^+$ start from the same position $A(0)=x$, we only have to check whether the left limits of the processes coincide. To this end, the left limits of $A(L(t))$ are
		\begin{align}
			A(L(t-))=\begin{cases}
				A(L(t)-),&\text{if $L(s)<L(t)$ for $s<t$},\\
