[Lem 10 of arXiv:2607.28728, '2607.28728'] For $x\in \R\setminus \{0\}$, we have
\begin{align*}
\lim_{t\to \infty}\sqrt{t}\mathbf{P}_{x}(\tau_{\{0\}}>t)=\sqrt{\frac{2}{\pi}}|x|.
\end{align*}

metadata_json:
{"artifact_digest":"sha256:ac81a8aa82922a5e241a87c60a5b06c5c7d6ffdb25a550eb68df5b5b23b1263c","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/ac/ac81a8aa82922a5e241a87c60a5b06c5c7d6ffdb25a550eb68df5b5b23b1263c.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:82a6004057dfd5921c0e8cbecf854505a28ef1f1f62fe4a772e13be8ee74f948","bundle_entry_key":"2607-28728-lemma-9-lem2-3","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_083","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_083.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-28728-lem-9-lem2-3","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $x>0$. It is known that the hitting-time process $(\tau_{\{x\}})_{x\ge 0}$ of a Brownian motion is a $\frac{1}{2}$-stable subordinator (see, e.g., \cite[Theorem 7.4.1]{Durrett}). Thus, we have
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{P}_{x}(\tau_{\{0\}}>t)&=\mathbf{P}(\tau_{\{x\}}>t)\\
&=\int_t^\infty \frac{x}{\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-\frac{x^2}{2s}}s^{-\frac{3}{2}}ds\\
&=\frac{x}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{t}}\int_1^\infty e^{-\frac{x^2}{2tu}}u^{-\frac{3}{2}}du\qquad (s=tu).
\stepcounter{equation}\tag{\theequation}
\end{align*}
