body:
[Proposition prop:extension.Luk.1994.Lemma.2.4 of arXiv:2606.04859, 'Stein's method for the Wishart distribution'] Let $\alpha\in (d - 1, \infty)$ and $\Sigma\in \mathcal{S}_{++}^d$ be given, and set $\Sigma_t \vcentcolon= (1 - e^{-2t})\Sigma$. For the matrix Wishart process defined in (equation eq:Wishart.process of the paper), with transition kernel on $\mathcal{S}_{+}^d$, for every initial state $W\in \mathcal{S}_{+}^d$ we have
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{W}_t \mid \{\mathfrak{W}_0 = W\} &\sim \mathcal{W}_d(\alpha, \Sigma_t, e^{-2t} \Sigma_t^{-1} W), \qquad t > 0,  \\
\mathfrak{W}_t \mid \{\mathfrak{W}_0 = W\} &\stackrel{\mathrm{law}}{\longrightarrow} \mathfrak{W}_{\infty} \sim \mathcal{W}_d(\alpha, \Sigma), \qquad t\to \infty. 
\end{align}
In particular, let $(\mathcal{P}^{\mathcal{W}}_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be the transition semigroup with kernel $P_t(W, \, \mathrm{d} Y)$ on $\mathcal{S}_{+}^d$, so that, for every bounded Borel measurable function $h:\mathcal{S}_{+}^d\to\mathbb{R}$,
\begin{equation}
(\mathcal{P}^{\mathcal{W}}_t h)(W) \vcentcolon= \int_{\mathcal{S}_{+}^d} h(Y) \, P_t(W, \, \mathrm{d} Y) = \mathsf{E}\big[h(\mathfrak{W}_t)\mid \mathfrak{W}_0 = W\big].
\end{equation}
When $h$ is only defined on $\mathcal{S}_{++}^d$, we use the same notation for $t > 0$ and $W\in \mathcal{S}_{+}^d$ by choosing any bounded Borel extension of $h$ to $\mathcal{S}_{+}^d$ since $P_t(W, \partial\mathcal{S}_{+}^d) = 0$. For $\mathfrak{S}_t\sim \smash{\mathcal{W}_d(\alpha, I_d, e^{-2t} \Sigma_t^{-1/2} W \Sigma_t^{-1/2})}$, the above shows
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{P}_t^{\mathcal{W}} h(W) = \mathsf{E}\big[h(\Sigma_t^{1/2} \mathfrak{S}_t \Sigma_t^{1/2})\big].
\end{equation}
Moreover, given a probability measure $\mu$ on $\mathcal{S}_{+}^d$, the push-forward measure $\mu \mathcal{P}^{\mathcal{W}}_t$ is defined by
\[
(\mu \mathcal{P}^{\mathcal{W}}_t)(A) \vcentcolon= \int_{\mathcal{S}_{+}^d} P_t(W, A) \, \mu(\mathrm{d} W), \qquad A\subseteq \mathcal{S}_{+}^d \text{ Borel}.
\]
Hence, for $\xi \vcentcolon= \mathcal{W}_d(\alpha, \Sigma)$, we have the invariance
\begin{equation}
\xi \mathcal{P}^{\mathcal{W}}_t = \xi.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 43 of arXiv:2512.09136, 'On the Green's functions and Martin boundary structure of a planar diffusion in a disconti'] Let $f :\R \longrightarrow \R^+$ be a continuous function and $\phi(z):= \int_\R e^{xz}f(x)dx$. Suppose that there are $a < 0 < b$ such that $\phi$ converges absolutely in $a<\Re(z)<b$. For $\delta > 0$ and $s \in \C$, let $G^+_\delta(s) = \{z \in \C, \Re(z) > 0, z\neq s, |\arg(z-s)| > \delta\}$ (where the complex argument is taken in $(-\pi,\pi]$). Suppose that there exists $0 \leq \delta< \pi/2$ such that
\begin{itemize}
\item Function $\phi$ has a holomorphic continuation on $G^+_\delta(b)$.
\item The following asymptotics hold: $$\phi(z) \underset{|z|\to + \infty\atop z \in G^+_\delta(b)}\longrightarrow 0.$$
\item There exists $\lambda \in \R$ and constants $c \in \C$, $A \neq 0$ such that
\begin{equation}(\phi(z) -c)(b-z)^\lambda \underset{z\to b\atop z\in G^+_\delta(b)}{\longrightarrow}A.\end{equation}
\end{itemize}
Then: $$f(x) \underset{x \to +\infty}{\sim}\frac{A}{\Gamma(\lambda)}e^{-bx}x^{\lambda - 1} $$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

