[Theorem thm:j fixed of arXiv:2607.01946, 'Second-order fluctuations for a phase transition in random partitions'] Under the notations above,
\begin{multline}
\pp{\frac{C_{j}(\floor{nt})-{\mathbb E}\pp{C_j(\floor{nt})\;\middle\vert\;{\mathcal P}}}{n^{\alpha/2}}, \frac{{\mathbb E}\pp{C_j(\floor{nt})\;\middle\vert\;{\mathcal P}} - \floor{nt}^\alpha p_j\topp\alpha S_\alpha}{n^{\alpha/2}}}_{t\in[0,1],j\in{\mathbb N}}\\
\Rightarrow\pp{\pp{\frac {S_\alpha}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}}^{1/2}\zeta_{\alpha,j}\topp1(t),
\pp{\frac {S_\alpha}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}}^{1/2}\zeta_{\alpha,j}\topp2(t)}_{t\in[0,1],j\in{\mathbb N}},
\end{multline}
in $(D[0,1]\times D[0,1])^{\mathbb N}$ with
\begin{align*}
\zeta\topp1_{\alpha,j}(t)&:=
\int_{{\mathbb R}_+\times\Omega'}\pp{\inddd{N'(tr) = j} - \mathbb P(N'(tr) = j)}{\rm d} M_\alpha({\rm d} r{\rm d}\omega'),\\
\zeta\topp2_{\alpha,j}(t)&:=\int_{{\mathbb R}_+\times\Omega'}\mathbb P(N'(tr) = j){\rm d} M_\alpha({\rm d} r{\rm d}\omega'),
\end{align*}
where $M_\alpha$ is a Gaussian random measure on $(0,\infty)\times\Omega'$ with control measure $\alpha r^{-\alpha-1}{\rm d} r{\rm d}\mathbb P'$ and $N'$ on $(\Omega',\mathbb P')$ is a standard Poisson process, and $M_\alpha$ is independent of $S_\alpha$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem theo:main of arXiv:2606.24793, '\LARGE Random sequential nearest-neighbor coloring on trees'] Consider the coloring process on $\mathcal{T}^{(\ell)}$ with a blue seed at the root and red seeds at the leaves constructed with the coupling described above. Then the coloring process converges a.s.\ in the local topology to the random coloring of $\mathcal{T}$ obtained by coloring the genealogical component of the root in blue and all other components in red. Furthermore, the sequence $(M_\ell)_{\ell\ge 1}$ converges a.s. to infinity and satisfies
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{\ell\to \infty} \mathbb{P}(M_{\ell}=\ell-1)=1.  
\end{equation}
The sequence $(m_\ell)_{\ell\ge 1}$ converges a.s. to a finite random variable $m_\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}
   \mathbb{P}(m_\infty=1)=\inf_{\ell\ge 1} \mathbb{P}(m_\ell=1)>0.  
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 11 of arXiv:2606.06859, 'The rectangular finite free heat flow'] If $r_1(t) > \cdots > r_d(t) > 0$ satisfy the ODE system (\ref{eq:ode}), then
\begin{equation} 
\sum_{k=1}^d{r_k(t)\dot{r}_k(t)} = d(n+d).
\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation} 
\sum_{k=1}^d{r_k(t)^2} = \sum_{k=1}^d{r_k(0)^2} + 2d(n+d)t
\end{equation}
for all $t\ge 0$, implying that the first moment of the root distribution of $p(x,t)$ grows linearly in time.

