body:
[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2606.06859, 'The rectangular finite free heat flow'] Let $p(x,t) = \prod_{i=1}^d (x - r_i(t)^2)$ be a polynomial of degree $d$ with nonnegative real roots that satisfies the partial differential equation
\begin{equation} 
{\partial_t} p(x,t) = -2\left( x {\partial_x^2} + (n+1) {\partial_x} \right) p(x,t).
\end{equation}
Then the Cauchy transform of the empirical root distribution \[
G(x,t) = \frac{1}{d}\sum_{i=1}^d \frac{1}{x - r_i(t)^2}
\]
satisfies the partial differential equation
\begin{equation} 
\partial_t G + 2 \partial_x \left( d x G^2 + (n+1) G + x \partial_x G \right) = 0.
\end{equation}

