body:
[Theorem thm111 of arXiv:2609.03091, 'Perfect numbers, Wieferich primes and the solutions of $\binom{2n}{n}\equiv 2^n \bmod n$'] Let $n=2^k p$, where $p$ is an odd prime and $k\in \mathbb{N}$, then $n$ is solution of the equation
\begin{equation} 
\binom{2n}{n} \equiv 2^n \bmod n
\end{equation}
if and only if $p$ satisfies the following conditions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item [a)] $p$ divides $\binom{2^{k+1}}{2^k}-2^{2^k}$;
    \item [b)] $p$ has at least $k$ digits $1$'s in its binary expansion.
\end{itemize}

