{"artifact_digest":"sha256:f67ec1ba2885fe641a7a875d9bb9731447bf41548ed7e3ff4a63dc53bf45545c","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/f6/f67ec1ba2885fe641a7a875d9bb9731447bf41548ed7e3ff4a63dc53bf45545c.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:7ed7820dced0fd15b99103b59bf8c9aac894ce17b85f6bd808c7ab0e951eaa2a","bundle_entry_key":"2608-24731-lemma-15-properties-of-one-round-loss-p","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_117","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_117.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-24731-lemma-15-properties-of-one-round-loss-p","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The common loss $\ell_{\boldsymbol{u}}$ is affine, and the rare loss $h_{\boldsymbol{u}, \alpha}$ is the maximum of two affine functions. Both are therefore continuous and convex. Since $\langle\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{x}\rangle\in[-1,1]$, both take values in $[0,1]$. By definition, we can verify $h_{\boldsymbol{u},\alpha}(\boldsymbol{u})=1$, and $h_{\boldsymbol{u},\alpha}(\boldsymbol{v})=0$ whenever $\langle\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{v}\rangle\leq\cos\alpha$.

It remains to prove the two inequalities. The definition of $p_\alpha$ gives
\[
  \frac{1-p_\alpha}{2}
  =\frac{p_\alpha}{1-\cos\alpha}
  =\frac{1}{3-\cos\alpha}.
\]
If $\langle\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{x}\rangle\leq\cos\alpha$, ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:aacee286dd05231837a33b564533a7cc61b7c27b4d5d5226de9ba66a66caec4c","revision_digest":"sha256:ddb931d19971706f1247f864d0483353a820dedb029fcb3daebde9069408c2b5","source_id":"arxiv:2608.24731","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

body:
[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2604.01978, 'Homogenized Transformers'] Fix $d, n\ge 2$ and $\upbeta\in[0,1]$, and assume that $\langle x_i(0),x_j(0)\rangle\ge \gamma$ for $i\neq j$ and $\gamma\in(0, 1)$. Define the empirical mean
\begin{equation*} 
m(t)\coloneqq\frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n \langle x_i(t),x_j(t)\rangle.
\end{equation*}
Let $u$ be the unique solution to the  Cauchy problem for the logistic equation
\begin{equation} 
\begin{cases}
\dot{u}(t)=u(t)(1-u(t)) \\
u(0)=m(0).
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Then there exists a universal constant $C>0$ such that for every every $T>0$ and every $\delta\in(0,1)$, with probability at least $1-\delta$, the solution to \eqref{eq:Diffusive_gaussian_case} satisfies
\begin{equation}
\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\left|m(t)-u(t)\right|
\le Ce^{CT}\max\left(\frac{1}{d},\,d^{2}\sigma_{\bA}^{4}\upbeta^{2}\right)
+ C\sqrt{\frac{T}{d}\log\left(\frac{4}{\delta}\right)}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 12 (Summed $L^2$ bound) of arXiv:2608.06925, 'Sharp asymptotics for the tree-completion time in\\ cylindrical Hastings--Levitov$(0)$'] With $m_N$ as in \eqref{eq:mN},
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{k\ge m_N}\EE Z_k^2\le(1+o(1))\,4\pi^2\sum_{k\ge m_N}q^{2k}=(1+o(1))\sum_{k\ge m_N}(\EE Z_k)^2 .
\end{equation}

[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2609.02104, 'Conformal Ricci Curvature and Spectral Estimates'] Let $\Omega\Subset M^4$ be smooth, bounded, and connected. Assume that $R_g>0$ and $\frac{Q_g}{R_g}\geq-\kappa$ on $\Omega$ for some $\kappa\in\mathbb R$. Then
\begin{equation}
\lambda_1(\Omega)
\leq
\frac{1}{25}
\left[
\sqrt{90\kappa+\frac{96\pi^2}{r_\Omega^2}}
+\frac{4\pi}{r_\Omega}
\right]^2.
\end{equation}
If $(M^4,g)$ is complete and noncompact, $R_g>0$ on $M$, $\kappa\geq0$, and $\frac{Q_g}{R_g}\geq-\kappa$ globally, then
\begin{equation}
\lambda_1(-\Delta_g)\leq\frac{18}{5}\kappa.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem_translate of arXiv:2507.06522, 'Balanced multi-species spin glasses'] Given $p,d\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geqslant1}$, let $N = p d$ and consider the multi-species model (equation MSKHamiltonian of the paper) with $\mathscr{S} = \{1,\dots,p\}$, $\# \mathcal{I}_{s,N} = d$ for each $s\in\mathscr{S}$, and 
\eeq{ 
\Delta^2_{s_1,\dots,s_q} = 0 \quad \text{whenever $q\ne p$}, \qquad
\Delta^2_{s_1,\dots,s_p} = \begin{cases}
\frac{p^{p+1}}{p!} &\text{if $s_1,\dots,s_p$ are distinct} \\
0 &\text{else.}
\end{cases}
}
Then the covariance function is
\eeq{ 
\xi_N(x_1,\dots,x_p) = px_1\cdots x_p, 
}
and we have the following distributional equality:
\eeq{ 
\frac{\|T\|_\mathrm{inj}}{\sqrt{d}} \stackrel{\mathrm{law}}{=} \max_{\sigma\in\Sigma_{N,\mathsf{spherical}}}\frac{H_{N}(\sigma)}{N}.
}

