[Theorem th:mainSPECTRUMgraph of arXiv:2510.00748, '\bf On irreducible central limit theorems'] Let $Z_n$ be as in (equation eq:bilformsubsec of the paper), and let $G_n=(V_n,\mathcal{E}_n)$ be its associated graph. Suppose that there exists $k\ge 2$ such that, as $n\to \infty$,
\begin{equation}
\liminf_n \mu_k^{(n)} >0.
\end{equation}
Then $\{Z_n\}$ is irreducible, in the sense of Definition (def:irreducibility of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm: construction of the continuous noise of arXiv:2602.04429, 'Disordered systems and (subcritical) polynomial chaos\\ with heavy-tail disorder'] {}
	$\boldsymbol\zeta^{(a)}$ converges almost surely in $H_{\rm loc}^{-s}(\Omega)$ for every $s >  \frac{D}{2}$. Its limit is denoted $\boldsymbol\zeta$ and its law is characterized by 
	\[ 
	\mathds{E}\Big[ \mathrm{e}^{i \langle \boldsymbol\zeta, \ensuremath{\mathbf{f}} \rangle} \Big] = \exp\bigg( \int_{\Omega \times {\ensuremath{\mathbb R}} } \Big( \mathrm{e}^{i \ensuremath{\mathbf{f}}(x) z} -1 -i \ensuremath{\mathbf{f}}(x) z) \mathrm{d} x \lambda(\mathrm{d} z) \bigg) \,,
	\]
	for every function $\ensuremath{\mathbf{f}} \in {\ensuremath{\mathcal D}} _0(\Omega)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 33 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] For all~$\lambda>0$, $\sigma\in(0,1/10)$ and~$\delta\in(0,1)$ there exist~$c>0$ and~$a_2\ge0$ such that for all $s\ge 1$, all~$u$ satisfying $s\ge u\ge s-\lambda$ and all~$n$ with~$s\le n^{1-\delta}$,
\begin{equation}

\begin{aligned}
Q_{n,s,u}\Biggl(\biggl(\,\bigcap_{k=1}^n\Bigl\{h(x_k)\le&\frac {n-k}n\wt m_n+s+\gamma_{n,a}(k)\Bigr\}\biggr)^\cc\,\Biggr)
\\
&\le
c\frac{(1+s-u)s}n\sum_{1\le k\le n/2}\bigl[k+\gamma_{n,a}(k)^2\bigr]\texte^{-\frac18 c_2\gamma_{n,a}(k)}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
holds for all~$a\ge a_2$ with
\begin{equation}

\gamma_{n,a}(k):=a+\bigl(\min\{k,n-k\}\bigr)^\sigma.
\end{equation}
Here~$c_2$ is as in \eqref{E:4.73}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2606.22044, 'Degenerate Stochastic Delay Modified Equation: Approximation of Stochastic Variance Reduce'] Let $Y_{t}$ be defined by (\ref{sfde-0-0}) and for any $g\in\mathcal{G}_{1}^{2},$ denote $P_{t}g(y)=\mathbb{E}[g(Y_{t}^{y})]$. Then, for arbitrary $y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$, whenever $\eta\in(0,1]$ and $t\in[0,m\eta]$, the following holds:
\begin{align}
|\nabla P_{t}g(y)|\leq e^{Cm\eta},
\end{align}
\begin{align}
\|\nabla^{2}P_{t}g(y)\|_{{\rm HS}}\leq C_{1}e^{C_{2}m\eta},
\end{align}
where $C,C_{1},C_{2}$ are positive constants, and are independent of $m$ and $\eta$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prp 8 ($L^p$ decay) of arXiv:2606.24167, '\bf On the stability of rarefaction for stochastic viscous conservation law'] Let $\phi\in X_1(T) $ be the solution of \eqref{perturbation}. Then for any $4< p< \infty$ 
	\begin{eqnarray}
		\mathbb{E} \| \phi \|_p^p \le C_{p}(1 + t)^{- \frac{p-2}{4}}\ln^\frac{p}{2}(2+t).
	\end{eqnarray}

