[Lemma lem:couplingtight of arXiv:2509.23086, 'On Optimal Markovian Couplings of L\'evy Processes'] Let $\mathcal{M},\mathcal{N}\subset \Lambda(\mathbb R^d)$ be two $\Lambda$-tight families, and define
	\begin{align*}
		\Gamma^\Lambda(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{N}) = \cb{\gamma\in\Lambda(\mathbb R^{2d}) : \gamma \in\Gamma^\Lambda(\mu,\nu),\ \mu\in\mathcal{M},\ \nu\in\mathcal{N} }.
	\end{align*}
	That is, $\Gamma^\Lambda(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{N})$ is the family of all L\'evy couplings $\gamma$ of $\mu\in\mathcal{M},\ \nu\in\mathcal{N}$. Then $\Gamma(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{N})$ is $\Lambda$-tight in $\Lambda(\mathbb R^{2d})$.

