[Proposition 32 of arXiv:2607.18201, 'Finding Adam in noisy trees'] For any $k > 1$ and $\alpha'>\alpha$, there exists a function $P(k,\alpha'-\alpha)$, depending only on $k$ and the difference $\alpha'-\alpha$, such that $P(k,\cdot)$ is monotone decreasing for any $k \geq 1$, and 
    $\lim_{\eps\to 0}P(k,\eps)=\infty$,
    such that for all large $n$,
    \begin{align*}
        \Exp{|\bC_\alpha(1)|^k}
        \leq P(k,\alpha'-\alpha)   n^{k\left(2\alpha'-\alpha\right)}.
    \end{align*}

