[Proposition 4 (Bayesian algorithmic lower envelope) of arXiv:2606.07931, 'Pointwise Complexity for Gaussian Fields:\\Upper Envelopes, Algorithmic Lower Bounds, and '] For every estimator \(\widehat t\), every \(\Delta>0\), and every reference law \(Q\) with \(0<\rho_{\Delta,Q}(\widehat t)<1\),
\begin{equation}
\mathbb P_{t\sim\pi,\,Y\sim P_t}\{d(t,\widehat t(Y))\ge\Delta\}
\ge
\left(
1-\frac{\mathcal I_{\pi}(Q)+\log 2}{\log(1/\rho_{\Delta,Q}(\widehat t))}
\right)_+ .
\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E_{t\sim\pi,\,Y\sim P_t}d(t,\widehat t(Y))
\ge
\sup_{\substack{\Delta>0,\,Q:\\ 0<\rho_{\Delta,Q}(\widehat t)<1}}
\Delta
\left(
1-\frac{\mathcal I_{\pi}(Q)+\log 2}{\log(1/\rho_{\Delta,Q}(\widehat t))}
\right)_+ .
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 1 (Letwin's quadratic and moment-map estimates) of arXiv:2608.27657, '\bfseries Stein Kernels and Normal Approximation\\ for Log-Concave Bilinear Forms'] The cited \cite[Theorems~1.2 and~2.5]{Letwin} state the following estimates.
For
every isotropic log-concave \(Z\in\R^n\) and every deterministic symmetric
matrix \(A\),
\begin{equation}
  \Var(Z^{\mathsf T}AZ)\leq8\Tr(A^2).
  \tag{2.7}
\end{equation}
In the regular moment-map model above, for every deterministic symmetric
matrix \(A\),
\begin{equation}
  \E\Tr(AHAH)\leq2\Tr(A^2).
  \tag{2.8}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition HBDG of arXiv:2607.28167, 'Temporal properties of the stochastic fractional heat equation with rough dependence in sp'] $($[HHLNT2017]$)$
   Let $W$ be the Gaussian noise with the covariance (equation CovStru of the paper), and let    $f\in\Lambda_H$  be a predictable random field. Then, we have   {that} for any $p\geq2$,
   \begin{equation}
     \begin{split}
        \left \|\int_{0}^{t}\int_{\mathbb{R}}f(s,y) W(ds,dy)\right\|_{L^p(\Omega)}
     \leq \sqrt{4p}c_{2,8}\left (\int_{0}^{t}\int_{\mathbb{R}}\left[\mathcal{N}_{\frac 12-H,\,p}f(s,y)\right]^2dyds\right)^{\frac 12},
     \end{split}
   \end{equation}
   where $c_{2,8}$ is a constant depending only on $H$ and
   $\mathcal{N}_{\frac 12-H,\,p}f(s,y)$ denotes the application of $\mathcal{N}_{\frac 12-H,\,p} $  to the spatial variable $y$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

