body:
[Theorem Theorem of intermediately cases of arXiv:2605.20800, 'On the maximal displacement of subcritical branching random walk in random environment'] Under (subcritical of the paper) and (Jump Assumption of the paper), assume $\alpha>1$ and
		$\varTheta(1)=0$, which implies that $\Lambda_e(1)<0$ and 
		\begin{equation}
			\varTheta(1)=\Lambda_e^\prime(1)+\Lambda_s(\lambda_1)-\lambda_1\Lambda_s^\prime(\lambda_1) =0.
		\end{equation}
		where $\lambda_1$ satisfies $ \Lambda_e(1)+\Lambda_s(\lambda_1) =0 $.
		In addition, we assume that there exists some $\delta>0$ such that
		\begin{equation}
			\mathbb{E}\left(e^{X_1-\Lambda_e(1)} (\log_+ \eta_1)^{2+\delta}\right)<\infty.
		\end{equation}
		Then there exists some finite and positive constant $C_2$, such that
		\begin{equation}
			\lim\limits_{x\rightarrow\infty}\sqrt{x}e^{\lambda_1 x }\mathbb{P}(M\geqslant x ) = C_2.
		\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

