[Theorem 3 (\cite{BS2024}) of arXiv:2604.00882, 'On some extensions of generalized counting processes'] The process ${M}^{\psi,f}$ has probability distribution function
   	 \begin{equation}
   	          \tilde{p}_n^{\psi,f}(t)=\sum_{\Omega(k,n)}^{}\prod_{j=1}^{k}\frac{\lambda_j ^{x_j}}{x_j!}\left(-\partial_{\Lambda}\right)^{z_k}\tilde{\ell}_f(t,\psi (\Lambda)), \, n\ge 0,
    	\end{equation}
    	and $\tilde{p}_n^{\psi,f}(t)$ satisfy the following equation
    	\begin{equation}
   		 \mathcal{D}^f_t \tilde{p}_n^{\psi,f}(t)=-\psi(\Lambda)\tilde{p}_n^{\psi,f}(t) -\sum_{m=1}^{n}\sum_{\Omega(k,m)}^{}\psi^{(z_k)}\left(\Lambda\right) \prod_{j=1}^{k}\frac{(-\lambda_j )^{x_j}}{x_j!}\tilde{p}_{n-m}^{\psi,f}(t),, \, n\ge 0, \,t>0,
  	\end{equation}
   	 with initial conditions 
    \begin{equation*}
         \tilde{p}_n^{\psi,f}(0)=
         \begin{cases}
                1,\quad n=0,\\
                0,\quad n\geq1.\\
         \end{cases}
     \end{equation*}
        The probability generating function of the process ${M}^{\psi,f}$ is of the form
\begin{equation}
             \tilde G^{\psi,f}(u,t)=\tilde \ell_f\Big(t, \psi\Big(\sum_{j=1}^{k}\lambda_j(1-u^j)\Big)\Big),\, |u|<1,
\end{equation}
and satisfies the equation
\begin{equation}
        	\mathcal{D}^f_t \tilde G^{\psi,f}(u,t)=-\psi\Big(\sum_{j=1}^{k}\lambda_j(1-u^j)\Big)\tilde G^{\psi,f}(u,t)
    \end{equation}
    with $\tilde G^{\psi,f}(u,0)=1$.
    The derivatives used in \eqref{Dtfpn} and \eqref{DftG} are the C-D convolution-type derivatives defined in \eqref{fDt}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem lem-disk-walk-conv of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] For $n\in \mathbbm N$, let $E_n$ be as in~(equation eqn-walk-cone-event of the paper). 
Let $\{l_n\}_{n\in\mathbbm N}$ be a sequence of positive integers. Assume there exists $A > 1$ such that $l_n \in [A^{-1} n^{1/2} , A n^{1/2}]$ for each $n\in\mathbbm N$. 
Let $\mathcal Z_n^{\operatorname{b}} = (\mathcal L_n^{\operatorname{b}} , \mathcal R_n^{\operatorname{b}})$ be a walk from $(l_n,0)$ to $(0,0)$ in $[0,\infty)^2$ sampled from the $\mathbbm P_{(l_n,0)}$-conditional law of $\mathcal Z$ given $E_n$. 
Also let $K_n$ be sampled uniformly from $[1,l_n]\cap\mathbbm Z$, independently from $\mathcal Z_n^{\operatorname{b}}$ and let $T_n$ be as in (equation eqn-disk-walk-time of the paper).

For each fixed $N\in\mathbbm N$, the law of
\begin{equation} 
\Big( \mathcal Z_n^{\operatorname{b}}(\cdot  + T_n) - \mathcal Z_n^{\operatorname{b}}(T_n) \Big) |_{[-N,N]} 
\end{equation}
converges in the total variation sense as $n\to\infty $ to the law of $\mathcal Z^{\operatorname{b}}|_{[-N,N]}$, where $\mathcal Z^{\operatorname{b}}$ is the encoding walk for the UIBHBOT, as defined at the beginning of Section~(sec-busemann-sym of the paper).

