{"artifact_digest":"sha256:75139cf84fe436c95880828b0dbdd29c24c79c52443a9a3e3318d781459d1171","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/75/75139cf84fe436c95880828b0dbdd29c24c79c52443a9a3e3318d781459d1171.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:b2880359d7fb18d92b1c3a17d9a84ddee24cdc27a0e8e543bc0411fadfe6b2b1","bundle_entry_key":"2604-26298-lemma-7-last-occurrence-scale-comparis","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_021","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_021.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2604-26298-lemma-7-last-occurrence-scale-comparis","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Set \(v=u/N\).  Since \(1\le u\le m-1\), we have \(0<v<\rho\).  Also
\(\tau/\rho=1-e^{-\tau}\).  We first compare the main continuous factors.
Using the saddle-point relation,
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\frac{e^{-v}e^{-\tau(1-v/\rho)}(1-e^{-\tau v/\rho})/(1-e^{-\tau})}
        {e^{-\rho}(1-e^{-v})}  \\
&\qquad
=\exp\{(\rho-\tau)+v(\tau/\rho-1)\}
  \frac{1-e^{-(1-e^{-\tau})v}}
       {(1-e^{-\tau})(1-e^{-v})}  \\
&\qquad
=\exp\{e^{-\tau}(\rho-v)\}
  \frac{1-e^{-(1-e^{-\tau})v}}
       {(1-e^{-\tau})(1-e^{-v})}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The exponential factor is bounded above and below by absolute constants,
because \(0<v<\rho\) and
\[
        \rho e^{-\tau}=\frac{\tau}{e^\tau-1}\le1.
\]
