[Proposition 13 of arXiv:2604.22567, 'Sign-balance of random Laplace eigenfunctions'] For every $\ell\ge 1$ and $\eta\in [1,\ell]$ one has 
\begin{equation}

K_{\ell,\eta}(0)\equiv 1.
\end{equation}
Further, for every $\ell\ge 1$, $\eta\in [1,\ell]$ and $\theta\in \left[0,\frac{\pi}{2}\right]$ one has
\begin{equation}

K_{\ell,\eta}(\theta)=c_{d}\cdot  \frac{1}{(\theta\ell)^{\frac{d-1}{2}}} \Big (\cos(\theta\ell+\gamma_{d}) + O\Big(\frac{1}{\ell \theta} + \eta \theta \Big)  \Big),
\end{equation}
where $\gamma_{d}$ is given by \eqref{eq:gammad offset def}, $$c_{d} = \frac{(d-1)!\cdot |\Sc^{d}|}{(2\pi)^{(d+1)/2}}$$ is a dimensional constant, and the constant implicit in the `$O$'-notation only depends on $d$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Thm 2 of arXiv:2605.05430, 'Dirichlet problems and exit distributions for the telegraph process and its planar extensi'] For $x\in[a,b]$, it holds that
\begin{equation}h_0(x)=\frac{\lambda}{c^2}\,(b-x)(x-a)+\frac{b-x}{c}\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}h_1(x)=\frac{\lambda}{c^2}\,(b-x)(x-a)+\frac{x-a}{c}\end{equation}
Consequently, \begin{equation}h(x)=\frac{\lambda}{c^2}\,(b-x)(x-a)+\frac{b-a}{2c}.\end{equation}

[Corollary 31 (Uniform defective LSI) of arXiv:2608.24863, 'Uniform Logarithmic Sobolev Inequalities for the 2D Coulomb Gas at the Diffusive Temperatu'] For every fixed $\beta>0$, every $N\ge2$, and every real-valued $f\in \mathsf D_{N,\beta}$,
\begin{equation}
 \Ent_{\mathbb P_{N,\beta}}(f^2)
 \le\frac4{\rho_{\rm one}(\beta)}\cE_{N,\beta}(f)
 +2\mathfrak D_\beta\int_{\C^N} f^2\,\D \mathbb P_{N,\beta}.
\end{equation}

