{"artifact_digest":"sha256:01493bb93f082c4a87f689c9075b27a211ba439598c0baaf75817e689de691e8","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/01/01493bb93f082c4a87f689c9075b27a211ba439598c0baaf75817e689de691e8.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:6dfc6007a8df781d09125f492dcccf14d7708dde09a4ec3265fdaa6178c78c83","bundle_entry_key":"pesin_stable_manifold_theorem","bundle_id":"population.pivot2-dynamics-b","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"smooth ergodic theory, non-uniform hyperbolicity, Pesin theory","draft":"claude-pivot2-dynamics-b/bundle_pivot2-dynamics-b.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:dynamics.smooth:pesin-stable-manifold-theorem","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Sketch (Hadamard-Perron graph transform in Lyapunov charts). Fix eps > 0 small compared with the gap between lambda^s(x) and 0. Using temperedness, choose along the orbit of a typical x a measurable family of linear changes of coordinates L_x : T_x M -> R^d (Lyapunov, or adapted, coordinates) in which the Oseledets subspaces are orthogonal and the derivative becomes uniformly contracting on the stable part: the norm of the restriction of the conjugated derivative to the stable factor is at most e^{lambda^s(x) + eps} and its inverse on the complementary factor is controlled by e^{-eps} factors; the price is that the norm of L_x is only tempered, so the chart is defined on a ball of radius r(x) with r tempered.

Pull f back through the exponential map and these charts to get maps F_n = L_{f^{n+1} x} composed with (exp^{-1} f exp) composed with L_{f^n x}^{-1}, defined on balls of radius r(f^n x) in R^d. Each F_n is the sum of a linear part with the stated contraction on the stable factor and a nonlinear remainder whose C^1 norm is at most const * r(f^n x)^a on that ball -- this is exactly where the Holder continuity of Df is used, and where a merely C^1 map gives no rate at all.

On the space of Lipschitz graphs over the stable factor with small Lipschitz constant, defined over the balls along the orbit, the graph transform (push a graph forward by F_n and take the graph of the image) is well defined for r small and is a contraction in the C^0 graph distance, because the stable directions contract by at least e^{lambda^s + eps} < 1 while the transverse directions expand relative to them. The sequence-space version of the contraction mapping principle (Perron's method along the orbit) yields a unique invariant family of graphs; their images under the charts are the local stable disks. Invariance (ii) is built into the construction, the exponential estimate (i) comes from the contraction rate in the adapted metric, translated back at the cost of the tempered factor C(x), and temperedness of r in (iii) is inherited from the tempered chart sizes. C^{1+a} regularity of the graphs follows by running the same contraction on the space of graphs with Holder derivative, using the Holder bound on the remainder. Uniformity and continuity on Pesin blocks (iv) hold because on a block all the above constants are uniform and the construction depends continuously on the orbit data.","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:d37b643574863d0d87862b211345cdbd8fa1472dd80b325dff599f991ba15499","revision_digest":"sha256:eabc8d2a11c5f96e9cd3d9d0ae8e4274abc626af17b4f59d7b9ce92e1510c03b","source_id":"wikipedia:Stable_manifold_theorem","source_revision":"as-read-2026-09-12","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

