[Thm 4 (Tenie {\cite[Theorem 1.4) of arXiv:2609.00758, 'Deformations of Compact Calabi--Yau and Fano Varieties with Isolated Singularities'] {Ten}}]
Let $X$ be as in Theorem \ref{thm: Ten1}. Suppose that its Du Bois complex $\ul\Om^\bullet_X$ satisfies the equality
\begin{equation}
\dim_\C \mathbb{H}^{n-1}(X,\ul\Om^2_X)\le\dim_\C \mathbb{H}^1(X,\ul\Om^{n-2}_X).
\end{equation}
Then there exist an open neighbourhood $\De$ of $0\in\C$ and a proper flat morphism $f:\cX\to\De$ with $f^{-1}(0)\cong X$ and such that the singularities of $f^{-1}(t), t\in\De\-\{0\},$ are $1$-rational. The singularities of $X$ which are not $1$-rational become non-singular under this. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition 9 of arXiv:2608.29984, 'On Cauchy Problems for Parabolic Equations with Rough Coefficients'] Given $f\in L^{1}_{loc}(\R^{d})$, we say $f\in BMO(\R^{d})$ provided
\begin{equation}
[f]_{BMO}:=\sup_{Q}\frac{1}{|Q|}\int_{Q}|f-(f)_{Q}|dx,\ (f)_{Q}=\frac{1}{|Q|}\int_{Q}f(y)dy,
\end{equation}
the supermum is taken in all cubes in $\R^{d}$.

