[Lem lemma:existence_Volterra_V of arXiv:2605.29073, 'Volterra clocks and their pure-jump limits: \\hitting times of curved boundaries'] Let $\lambda \,, \nu\geq 0\,$, $f$ satisfying Assumption (assumption:f of the paper), $K \in L^2_{loc}(\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}_+\,, \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}_+)$ a completely monotone kernel, and $g_0 \in \mathcal G_K\,$. Then, there exists a continuous, nonnegative weak solution $V$ to 
    \begin{equation}

    Y_t = g_0(t) + \int_0^t\,K(t-s)\,\left(-\lambda\,Y_s\,ds + \nu\,\sqrt{f'(X_s)\,Y_s}\,dB_s \right)\,, \quad t \leq T\,,
    \end{equation}
    where $X_t := \int_0^t\,Y_s\,ds\,$. Additionally, $\sup_{t \leq T} \,\mathbb E \left[Y_t^p \right]$ is finite for all $p \geq 2\,$.

[Lemma of arXiv:2411.17653, 'Exclusion processes with non-reversible boundary: hydrodynamics and large deviations'] Let $L:C^{0,1}(\Omega_T)\to\mathbb{R}$ be any linear functional and recall 
    \begin{equation*}
    \mathfrak{M}(H):=\int_0^T\int_0^1\sigma(\rho_t)|\nabla H_t|^2\;dx\;dt+\int_0^T\Psi(t,H_t(0),H_t(1))\;dt.
\end{equation*}
Then 
\begin{equation*}
    \sup_{C^{0,1}(\Omega_T)}\{L_0(H)-\mathfrak{M}(H)\}=S_1+S_2,
\end{equation*}
where
\begin{equation*}
    S_1=\sup_{C^{0,1}_0(\Omega_T)}\{L_0(G)-\int_0^T\int_0^1\sigma(\rho_t)|\nabla G_t|^2\;dx\;dt\}
\end{equation*}
and 
\begin{equation*}
    S_2=\sup_{h,g\in C([0,T])}\{L(g_t[1-\Xi_t])+L(h_t\Xi_t)-\int_0^T\zeta_t[h_t-g_t]^2\;dt-\int_0^T\Psi(t,g_t,h_t)\;dt\}.
\end{equation*}
In the above formula, $\zeta_t=1/\langle 1/\sigma(\rho_t) \rangle$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma l.formula_cts_incre of arXiv:2411.14105, 'Simultaneous replica-symmetry breaking \\ for vector spin glasses'] Under~(i.setting of the paper), fix $X_0=0$. 
If $\alpha^{-1}$ is absolutely continuous on some interval $I\subseteq (0,1)$ and $L_p$ and $L_q$ are differentiable on $\left\{\alpha^{-1}(s):\:s\in I\right\}$, then $\overrightarrow{p}$ and $\overrightarrow{q}$ are differentiable at almost every $s\in I$ and satisfy
\begin{align}
    \dot \overrightarrow{p}(s) = 2\mathbb{E} \left[A_{\alpha^{-1}(s)}^\intercal \dot \overrightarrow{q}(s)A_{\alpha^{-1}(s)}\right].
\end{align}

